<p>Aaaaah. I never thought of it like that….I tend to write in the third person specifically so that I can sculpt and carve to my heart's content, because I sound so awkward and improvised in real life, and I don't want to do that when I write.</p>
<p>But, you're right, the awkwardness does lend credibility to the characters, and I cannot believe I have never noticed that before.</p>
<p>There aren't that many "warts", really…the only stuff I noticed on the first read was the stuff at the beginning, and I'm <em><strong>incredibly</strong></em> pedantic anyway. Moreover, the number of sentences and paragraphs I corrected does not compare to the number of sentences that were awesome.</p>

